{
 "title": "Fujichrome Provia 100F [RDP III] \u2014 per-layer spectral dye density",
 "family": "Fujichrome colour reversal (E-6 dye set)",
 "stocks_covered": [
  "Provia 100F (RDP III)"
 ],
 "normalization": "peak = 1.0 per dye (unit-normalized, NOT a neutral-forming set)",
 "units": "relative diffuse spectral density",
 "source": "Fujifilm Provia 100F datasheet AF3-036E p.6, '21. SPECTRAL DYE DENSITY CURVES'. The chart is embedded as FOUR overlaid 300 dpi 1-bit CCITT stencils: xref 55 = frame/gridlines/labels, xref 56 = yellow, 57 = magenta, 58 = cyan. Each curve is its own isolated single connected component, so no crossing/occlusion disambiguation was required.",
 "method": "Per-column ink-extent midpoint on each isolated curve layer. Axis calibration by least-squares over all four wavelength gridlines (400/500/600/700 nm at x=163/414/656/904) and all three density gridlines (0.0/0.5/1.0 at y=628/381/127); labels OCR-verified.",
 "registration_audit": "100% of traced points land on ink (median distance 0.00 px); 100% of curve ink covered within 6 px of a traced point, all three layers.",
 "uncertainty": "Gridlines are NOT evenly spaced in the source artwork (spacings 251/242/248 px where even spacing would give 247): the linear axis fit leaves residuals up to 3.0 px = 1.2 nm in wavelength and 2.3 px = 0.005 D in density. This is drawing/rasterisation imprecision in the datasheet itself, not a tracing error, and is the wavelength-scale uncertainty of this dataset. At 300 dpi, 1 px = 0.41 nm.",
 "important_note": "CYAN TRUNCATION IS THE DOMINANT SYSTEMATIC. All three curves are drawn to the plot's right edge (~718-719 nm), but cyan is still at 0.587 D there and falling; its true behaviour beyond 719 nm is unpublished. Per the project's no-synthesised-spectra rule the data is set to 0 beyond support rather than extrapolated. Consequence: ISO Status A red responsivity carries 0.020% of its weight beyond 719 nm, which the model therefore treats as CLEAR film. At low dye this is negligible (<=0.01 D for cyan<=2.5), but it is strongly NONLINEAR in cyan amount and creates a spurious hard ceiling on modelled Status A red at -log10(0.0002) = 3.708 D. Bracket on Status A red density (model = lower bound; upper bound = cyan held flat at its 718 nm value): cyan 2.5 -> 0.010 D, 3.0 -> 0.024 D, 3.5 -> 0.059 D, 4.0 -> 0.135 D. Sign is known: physical red reads HIGHER than model. NOTE this also applies to the Velvia 100 dataset (edge 710 nm, cyan 0.291 there; bracket 0.015 D at cyan 3.0, 0.081 D at cyan 4.0) - the earlier 'negligible, 0.00004 D per unit cyan' characterisation of that systematic is only valid at low cyan and understates it in deep shadow.",
